### Postgres Build Instructions
```
// configure the build
./configure --enable-debug --enable-cassert --prefix /tmp/pgsql

// build using parallel -j flag
make -j$(nproc) -s

// install into prefix directory
make install

// put prefix directory in your path
export PATH=/tmp/pgsql/bin:$PATH

// run initdb to initialize the database directory
initdb -D /tmp/pgdata

// start the database
pg_ctl -D /tmp/pgdata -l /tmp/pgdata/logfile start

// now you should be able to connect via psql client
psql postgres
```

### Extension Build Instructions
```
// make sure you're in the correct extension directory
cd contrib/blackhole_am/

// compile and install
make -j$(nproc) && make install

// to recompile your incremental changes and install
make install
```
Note that to reflect your changes after `make install`, you need to
exit (Ctrl-d) your existing psql session and open a new one to reload
the newly updated extension.

### Task
Modify this blackhole_am contrib module to make the following three
queries work:
1. CREATE TABLE
2. INSERT
3. SELECT

So for example, something like this should be expected at the end:
```
postgres=# CREATE EXTENSION blackhole_am;
CREATE EXTENSION
postgres=# CREATE TABLE foo (a int) USING blackhole_am;
CREATE TABLE
postgres=# SELECT * FROM foo;
 a
---
(0 rows)

postgres=# INSERT INTO foo VALUES (42);
INSERT 0 1
postgres=# SELECT * FROM foo;
 a
----
 42
(1 row)

postgres=# INSERT INTO foo VALUES (1), (23);
INSERT 0 2
postgres=# SELECT * FROM foo;
 a
----
 42
  1
 23
(3 rows)
```

For the INSERT calls, you'll need to store the input values in some
form of storage. Simply use a FILE implementation using the stdio
library or however you'd like (e.g. in-memory array).  So for example,
the above `foo` table may simply exist on disk as such:
```
$ cat /tmp/blackhole_am_16402
42
1
23
```

Optionally, try making DELETE and UPDATE work as well (or explain it
verbally if time allows).

Good luck!